  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1125 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nA deep plume of moisture extends from south central Colorado into\nwestern New Mexico at the time of this AFD, and significant\nrainfall totals have been reported across much of southwest and\nsouthern Colorado over the past 24 hours. In our forecast area,\nthere's been some scattered showers across the mountains and over\nthe eastern plains (mainly Lincoln and Washington counties),\nthough precipitation amounts have been very light so far. Guidance\ndoes attempt to push some of that more organized precipitation\ninto our forecast area overnight, but the terrain blocking and\ndownslope off the Front Range will likely prevent any meaningful\nrainfall from falling. The NAM is the only real exception to this\nbut doesn't appear to be a very realistic scenario. A few light \nshowers will continue at times overnight with mostly cloudy skies \nacross the forecast area.\n\nFriday will likely end up as the warmest (and breeziest) day of\nthe week, as the primary shortwave associated with the trough to \nour west will lift off to the northwest. The flow aloft should \nshift to a southwesterly direction, finally shunting the deeper \nmoisture plume off to the east of the state and advecting in the \nmuch drier airmass currently over Arizona. There would still be\njust enough moisture aloft to support a couple virga showers in \nour forecast area, but most of the day will feature partly cloudy \nskies. Mid-level temperatures will still be warm and the weak \nsubsidence and downslope should promote highs in the mid 70s to \nmid 80s across most of the lower elevations. A broad lee trough \nextending from Denver through eastern Wyoming will keep winds a \nbit lighter for the northern I-25 corridor, while most of the \nDenver metro and eastern plains see some gusts of 30-40 mph during\nthe afternoon hours. While we will see a drying trend, relative \nhumidity values should remain high enough to avoid any critical \nfire weather conditions during the afternoon hours.\n\nSaturday will feature quite the change from this period of warmer\nweather. A cold front still looks likely to arrive just after\nsunrise on Saturday morning, with steady northeast surface winds\nexpected through the day. As mentioned in numerous forecast\ndiscussions prior to this one, a cutoff low is expected to develop\nover the desert southwest (near the CA/AZ border) and lift into\nsoutheast Utah by late Saturday night. We'll see another period of\nmoisture advection into the area with stronger south-southwest\nflow redeveloping during the day, leading to an increase in cloud\ncover and chances for showers by late Saturday afternoon. Guidance\ncontinues to hint at a near-surface dry layer that would prevent\nsignificant precipitation across most of the mountains and\nadjacent plains, with better chances for rain across far eastern\nColorado in the better warm air advection/isentropic lift Saturday\nevening/night.\n\nThere is a bit more uncertainty for Sunday, as guidance begins to\nsplit into two scenarios. Most of the extended range CAMs and\nregional models keep moisture/stratus across the forecast area,\nwhile some deterministic guidance (ECMWF) advects a drier airmass\nback into the region. Those drier solutions are much warmer with\nhighs back in the 60s... while the moist/stratus solutions would\nkeep highs closer to 50F. The current forecast is a bit of a\ncompromise with a slight preference towards the cooler solutions\nat this time. The upper low is expected to shear out as it lifts\nthrough our forecast area Sunday night, but this could provide\nanother period of weak ascent... and guidance generally attempts\nto produce some light rain and snow showers over the region Sunday\nnight into Monday morning.\n\nMonday will be cool but generally dry in the wake of the trough, \nwith light flow aloft. The flow should turn southwesterly and \nbegin to strengthen by Tuesday or Wednesday ahead of the next \nwestern CONUS trough. That approaching trough could lead to \nanother period of cooler/unsettled weather across the state by \nlate next week or next weekend... but that falls outside our \ncurrent forecast period.",
